course: dictionary
lesson: 3023
title: Understanding dyslexia
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Dyslexia is a common difference in how the brain works with words. It makes reading and spelling harder, even for clever people. «Dyslexia is a difference that makes reading and spelling harder; it has nothing to do with being clever, and many very successful people have it.» So dyslexia is not about being lazy or unintelligent.
explain: Dyslexia is a lasting difficulty, but it is not a single mistake. «Dyslexia is a lasting difficulty with reading and spelling; only a specialist can identify it, never from one mistake.» Words like lasting and identify are clues: a difficulty that stays over time, and a specialist who finds it out.
explain: Context helps here too. «Mixing up b and d is common up to about age seven and does not by itself mean dyslexia.» The words up to about age seven tell you that young children often make this mistake, so one mix-up is not proof of dyslexia.
fact: Dyslexia is a common difference in how the brain works with words.
fact: Someone who has dyslexia is described as dyslexic.
fact: Dyslexia has nothing to do with being clever or lazy.
fact: Many inventors, artists and business people are dyslexic.
fact: Helpful things include coloured overlays, clear fonts, extra time in tests, audiobooks and multi-sensory spelling.
exercise: «Dyslexia is a difference that makes reading and spelling harder; it has nothing to do with being clever.» According to this sentence, dyslexia has nothing to do with being what? | Sporty | Tall | *Clever
exercise: «Only a specialist can identify it, never from one mistake.» What does this sentence say about spotting dyslexia? | Anyone can spot it from one mistake | It is found by reading one book | *It needs a specialist, not one mistake
exercise: «Mixing up b and d is common up to about age seven and does not by itself mean dyslexia.» Does mixing up b and d always mean dyslexia? | Yes, always | Only when someone is older than ten | *No, it is common up to about age seven
test: Which of these is a helpful thing for someone with dyslexia? | Shouting the words louder | Skipping every test | Hiding the book from them | *Audiobooks
test: What is a «dyslexia-friendly font»? | A font that is hard to read | A type of football | *A clear font that is easier to read
exam: Someone who has dyslexia is described as what? | Clever | *Dyslexic | Lazy
